--TEST--
`putSecret` seals under the ring's newest key and `getSecret` opens against every key of it, identically on the tiers that hold their own entries: a secret written before a rotation still reads, and the third tier takes the same pair — `rule:security/secret-crosses-no-boundary`, `rule:security/protocol-roster`, `rule:core-api/shape-rules`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Cache\Store::putSecret
// The two members are the only door through which a secret meets a cache, and
// what reaches a tier is ciphertext — so `rule:security/secret-crosses-no-boundary`
// is untouched and no `secret` value crosses the copy at all.
secret bytes $newest = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
secret bytes $retired = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $ring = [$newest, $retired];

secret string $token = "hunter2";

var $local = Core\Cache::local();
var $process = Core\Cache::process();

// `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R12: the lifetime is a `Core\Time\Duration`, and
// required here where `put`'s is an option — a secret never outlives a lifetime
// someone stated.
$local->putSecret("session-token", $token, 1h, $ring);
$process->putSecret("session-token", $token, 1h, $ring);

// Counted over both tiers rather than read off a line: a member that sealed on
// one tier and opened on the other by accident would still print a plausible
// row for the tier it got right.
int $opened = 0;
if (($local->getSecret("session-token", $ring) ?? "") == $token) {
    $opened = $opened + 1;
}
if (($process->getSecret("session-token", $ring) ?? "") == $token) {
    $opened = $opened + 1;
}
echo "a secret round-trips on the tiers that hold their own: ", $opened, " of 2", "\n";

// Which end of the ring is *newest* is the whole of what a program can get
// wrong here, and it is the same answer `Core\SignedCookie` gives: a ring
// holding only the front key still opens what was sealed under the full one.
array<secret bytes> $newestOnly = [$newest];
echo "sealed with the newest key: ",
    ($local->getSecret("session-token", $newestOnly) ?? "") == $token ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// The other half of rotation. An entry sealed before the key moved down the
// ring still opens, so a rotation costs no reader anything until the entry
// expires on its own — and a ring that has dropped that key is a miss rather
// than a failure, because a rotated ring is meant to re-fetch.
array<secret bytes> $retiredOnly = [$retired];
$local->putSecret("older", $token, 1h, $retiredOnly);
echo "a key still in the ring opens what it sealed: ",
    ($local->getSecret("older", $ring) ?? "") == $token ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
echo "and a ring that dropped it is a miss: ",
    $local->getSecret("older", $newestOnly) == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";

// The third tier takes the same two members to a store that keeps its own
// clock. A deployment that configured none is told so by name, exactly as it is
// for `put` and `get`.
try {
    Core\Cache::shared();
    echo "shared handed back a store", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo "the shared tier takes the same pair, and this deployment has no store", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
a secret round-trips on the tiers that hold their own: 2 of 2
sealed with the newest key: yes
a key still in the ring opens what it sealed: yes
and a ring that dropped it is a miss: null
the shared tier takes the same pair, and this deployment has no store
